LEGAL NOTICE
=============

rar-rs is a clean-room Rust implementation of the RAR archive format for
software conservancy and educational purposes, providing BSD-2-Clause licensed
`rar` and `unrar` command-line tools.

Clean-Room Implementation
--------------------------

The RAR5 LZSS+Huffman compression codec (src/codec/) was developed as a
clean-room implementation. The bitstream format was derived from analysis of
libarchive's archive_read_support_format_rar5.c by Grzegorz Antoniak (2018),
which is itself an independent BSD-2-Clause licensed implementation of a RAR5
decompressor. No proprietary RARLAB source code was used or referenced.

The RAR4 decompressor (src/rar4/) was developed as a clean-room implementation
based on libarchive's archive_read_support_format_rar.c, which is an
independent BSD-2-Clause licensed RAR4 reader. The RAR4 support is read-only:
it parses RAR4 headers, decompresses LZSS+Huffman (method 3) streams, and
applies VM filters (E8, E8E9, Delta, RGB, Audio). No RAR4 compression or
archive creation capability is provided.

This project does not contain, link to, or derive from the proprietary unrar
source code distributed by RARLAB under its restrictive license. The archive
header and block structure implementations are based on the publicly documented
RAR format specifications and libarchive's independent BSD-2-Clause work.

Portions from the `rars` project
--------------------------------

The RAR5 recovery-record codec (`src/recovery/rar5.rs`), the BLAKE2sp hash
(`src/blake2sp.rs`) and the RAR5 key-derivation / hash-key MAC patterns
(`src/encryption.rs`) are ported from the `rars` project
(https://github.com/bitplane/rars, crates.io `rars`), which is licensed
MIT OR Apache-2.0. Those portions retain their upstream license; the rest
of this project remains BSD-2-Clause.

Further `rars` portions ported later (same MIT OR Apache-2.0 terms; the
vendored repository copy carries the WTFPL licence with a no-blame clause):
the automatic x86 filter scan (`src/codec/filters.rs`,
`auto_x86_filter_ranges`), the binary-tree match finder
(`src/codec/match_finder.rs`, LZMA-style BT4), the optimal parse
(`src/codec/rar50.rs`, `optimal_parse_tokens` + `TokenPrices` +
`BlockSplitter`), the E8/E8E9 x86 transform modulo normalization, and the
RAR 3.x/4.x member decoders (`src/codec/rar29.rs`, decode half of rars
`codec/rar29.rs`; `src/codec/ppmd.rs`, decode half of rars `codec/ppmd.rs`).
Each carries a note at its definition site.

The `rar300/` test fixtures under `crates/rar/tests/fixtures/rar40/` are
legacy RAR 3.0 archives copied from the rars test corpus (same licence
terms), used as byte-level regression inputs.

Official RAR/UNRAR binaries are used strictly as black-box interoperability
references for testing (set `SA_OFFICIAL_RAR` / `SA_OFFICIAL_UNRAR` to run
the `official_*` interop tests); their code is not used or referenced, and
this project remains a clean-room implementation.

Scope and Limitations
----------------------

This software:

  - Does NOT supersede, replace, or replicate the full capabilities of the
    native RAR archiver produced by RARLAB (Alexander Roshal). The proprietary
    RAR encoder uses advanced techniques (including optimal parsing, context
    modelling, and PPMd) that this implementation does not attempt to reproduce.
    Compression ratios will vary; this implementation may produce larger output
    than the native tool in many real-world scenarios.

  - Provides RAR5 read/write (compress and decompress) and RAR4 read-only
    (decompress only) support. There is no ability to create or compress RAR4
    or RAR3 archives — only RAR5 archive creation is supported. RAR4 support
    is limited to reading, listing, testing, and extracting existing archives.

  - Is intended for software conservancy, interoperability, and educational use.
    It enables reading and writing of the RAR archive format without depending
    on proprietary binaries.

